Reproducibility and transparency
Live taxonomic databases change. Analyses using retrieved lineages should therefore record:

the taxodist version and implementation;
the retrieval date;
numeric Taxonomicon IDs, particularly for homonyms;
unresolved or excluded taxa;
whether live data or the packaged taxobase reference object was used;
the distance metric and downstream analysis settings.

The fixed taxobase object is distributed for examples, tests, and comparisons that must not depend on current server availability. It should not be assumed to represent the latest live classification.

The R, Python, and Julia packages are separate implementations of the same taxonomic hierarchy distance. Version 1.0.0, the first stable release, is published simultaneously in all three, and their shared behavioral contract states that:

lineages and node ranks are read by the same structured parser, checked against the same trimmed copies of real Taxonomicon pages and their expected lineages;
ordered lineages are compared through their continuous common prefix;
identical complete lineages receive distance zero;
by default, distinct nodes receive \(1 / \operatorname{depth}(\operatorname{MRCA})\);
the alternative metrics "path", "wu_palmer", and "ranked" have the same definitions and default rank levels;
valid Taxonomicon nodes have reciprocal distances in \((0,1]\), while disconnected inputs retain infinite distance as a defensive result;
unresolved lineages produce missing values in multi-taxon results;
ancestor–descendant pairs retain positive distance;
clade membership uses exact, case-insensitive matching;
the packaged reference data preserve the same component structure, taxa, order, values, and provenance;
resolution audits use the same fields and status vocabulary;
resolution records and name-based retrieval truncate lineages by the same rule, after the same whitespace normalization and case-insensitive location of the queried name;
coverage checks distinguish found, absent, and unknown (server unreachable);
portable bundles use the same schema version 1.1 and matrix encoding, and all three still read schema 1.0;
classification trees use the same topology rules and branch lengths, so their patristic distances equal the distance matrix in every implementation;
taxonomic diversity and distinctness indices are computed from the same formulas and agree with vegan::taxondive().

Compatibility means equivalent scientific semantics and labeled results. It does not mean that every object has the same internal representation or printed appearance.

