Portable bundle interoperability
Bundles (introduced in version 0.8.0) are a neutral JSON record for transferring complete analyses rather than language-specific cache internals. A bundle contains:

schema and creation timestamps;
source name, URL, and retrieval timestamp;
software name, version, and language;
metric name and definition, root-depth convention, and, for "ranked", the rank levels;
complete resolution records, candidate decisions, and lineage ranks;
labeled distance matrix values.

The current schema is version 1.1; version 1.0 bundles (written by 0.8.0 and 0.9.0) are still read and must use the reciprocal metric.
The shared encoding uses JSON null for missing distances and the strings "Infinity" and "-Infinity" for disconnected infinities. Each reader validates imported data before constructing native objects.
This enables a resolution performed in one implementation to be inspected and analyzed in another without repeating live taxonomy requests. compare_bundles() accepts bundles written by any implementation, so an analysis performed in one language can be compared with a later repetition in another.

Live retrieval may differ over time
The packages query an external website. Two runs can differ if they occur at different times, resolve different homonyms, encounter different network responses, or use different package versions.
For reproducible comparisons:

use the same taxodist version;
query the same numeric Taxonomicon IDs;
record the retrieval date;
verify lineage equality before comparing distances;
save language-specific caches when live records must be retained;
use taxobase for fixed examples and regression tests.

What compatibility does not guarantee
The project does not guarantee:

byte-identical serialized files;
identical console messages;
identical plot appearance;
identical internal clustering arrays;
identical eigenvector signs;
identical live results obtained from different source revisions.

It does guarantee a shared versioned definition and aims to preserve equivalent labels, distances, MRCA logic, edge-case handling, and reference data.

taxodist compares taxa through their positions in a taxonomic classification. Given a scientific name or a Taxonomicon numeric ID, it retrieves the corresponding ordered lineage from The Taxonomicon.
You can then use those lineages to:

calculate the distance between two hierarchy nodes, under the default reciprocal distance or a path-length, Wu–Palmer, or rank-based alternative;
identify their most recent common ancestor (MRCA);
inspect the rank of every lineage node;
construct pairwise distance matrices for communities;
compute taxonomic diversity and distinctness indices;
inspect shared and divergent portions of lineages;
test or filter clade membership;
perform hierarchical clustering and principal coordinates analysis (PCoA);
save retrieved lineages for later reuse.

Names at different taxonomic levels are accepted. Examples include "Tyrannosaurus", "Tyrannosaurus rex", "Dinosauria", "Homo sapiens", and "Drosophilidae".






Terminology



Throughout this site, distance means distance between nodes in a taxonomic hierarchy. It does not mean phylogenetic branch length or elapsed evolutionary time. Root depth is 1, so distinct valid Taxonomicon nodes have distances in \((0,1]\).

