Terminology



Throughout this site, distance means distance between nodes in a taxonomic hierarchy. It does not mean phylogenetic branch length or elapsed evolutionary time. Root depth is 1, so distinct valid Taxonomicon nodes have distances in \((0,1]\).

Dependence on the source classification
The distance depends on the number and arrangement of nodes recorded before the MRCA. It is therefore sensitive to:

taxonomic revision;
alternative classification systems;
insertion or removal of intermediate clades;
unequal resolution among taxonomic groups;
lineage-parsing and cleaning decisions (the structured parser of version 1.0.0 changed lineage depths, and therefore distances, for many taxa compared with earlier versions);
ambiguous name resolution.

Adding an intermediate node to the shared prefix increases MRCA depth and decreases the numerical distance. Adding nodes only below the MRCA does not affect that pair.
Distances should consequently be compared only when derived from compatible lineage sources and processing rules.

Interpretation
A smaller finite value indicates a deeper shared node in the selected classification. It does not quantify:

elapsed evolutionary time;
nucleotide or amino-acid substitution;
morphological divergence;
phylogenetic branch length;
probability or support for common ancestry;
ecological or functional similarity.

A clustering dendrogram constructed from these values summarizes the classification-depth matrix. Conversion to class phylo changes the data representation, not the evidential basis of the tree.

Reproducibility
Live lineages may change when The Taxonomicon is updated. For empirical work, record:

taxodist version and implementation;
retrieval date;
queried names and resolved numeric IDs;
treatment of homonyms and redirects;
missing or excluded taxa;
lineage source and cleaning settings;
the distance metric (and, for "ranked", the rank levels);
downstream clustering, ordination, or permutation settings.

Use save_cache() when the exact retrieved lineages must be reused. Cache files are language-specific (.rds, .pkl, and .json). Use taxobase or load_taxobase() when a fixed, packaged example is preferable to a live query. When an analysis is repeated, compare_bundles() identifies which lineages, identifiers, and distances changed between the two retrievals.

Recommended reporting
A reproducible analysis should state:

the taxodist version and programming language;
the lineage source and retrieval date;
resolved Taxonomicon IDs and treatment of homonyms;
taxa excluded because of missing or disconnected lineages;
the distance metric (and, for "ranked", the rank levels) and its classification-depth interpretation;
clustering or ordination settings;
the permutation scheme and number of permutations;
checks for multivariate dispersion where PERMANOVA is used;
whether analyses used live lineages or the fixed taxobase object.
